1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
It's man versus nature in a battle to hold back water.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Massive earthen structures hundreds of miles long and monumental feats of engineering know-how.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Vitally important, though often taken for granted.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Until disaster strikes.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Levees on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,580
The images are all too familiar.

7
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,240
Flooding on a mass scale.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,320
Staggering property damage.

9
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:51,560
Human suffering.

10
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:54,740
And death.

11
00:00:54,741 --> 00:01:00,200
eventually a storm was going to overwhelm those levees in new orleans

12
00:01:00,201 --> 00:01:04,200
those levees lost the battle of too much water too fast

13
00:01:04,201 --> 00:01:08,800
when katrina decimated new orleans hurricane defense system

14
00:01:08,801 --> 00:01:12,740
levees became tragic symbols for what can go wrong

15
00:01:12,741 --> 00:01:15,240
when we think we can outsmart mother nature

16
00:01:15,241 --> 00:01:20,340
there are two kinds of levees those that have failed and those that will fail

17
00:01:20,341 --> 00:01:23,260
and anybody who tells you they can engineer fail-safe levees

18
00:01:23,261 --> 00:01:24,780
is not giving you the whole truth.

19
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:29,480
The forces that came together to produce the flood disaster in New Orleans

20
00:01:29,481 --> 00:01:31,000
are vastly complex.

21
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:36,540
But levees themselves are relatively simple in design and purpose.

22
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:43,200
Whether made of soil, stone, or steel, levees are built to hold back water.

23
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:48,660
The most common levees are massive earthen mounds.

24
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:52,760
As people began developing areas near rivers,

25
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:56,040
They just piled up dirt to defend against those rivers.

26
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:59,380
And that process is what we call a levee.

27
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:02,420
So a levee, fundamentally, is a pile of dirt.

28
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:08,320
To withstand the kind of extreme pressure water can exert,

29
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:11,880
levees need to be ten times as wide as they are high.

30
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:18,540
The point at which the base of the levee meets the land, or river bottom, is called the toe.

31
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:22,520
The sides of levees taper up to a level top.

32
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:25,640
or crown, usually capped by a road.

33
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,760
Levies are found wherever people have chosen

34
00:02:31,761 --> 00:02:33,840
to live or farm on floodplains,

35
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:38,360
or along coastal areas prone to large storms,

36
00:02:38,361 --> 00:02:39,400
like hurricanes.

37
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:46,660
The majority of levies are built out of layers

38
00:02:46,661 --> 00:02:49,640
of sediment or clay, taken from areas adjacent

39
00:02:49,641 --> 00:02:51,800
to rivers, called borrow pits.

40
00:02:52,760 --> 00:03:02,020
Concrete levees with deep foundations would be stronger, but they'd also be unattractive, bad for the environment, and much more expensive.

41
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:09,040
Because water always finds the weakest point, levees are prone to failure.

42
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:19,180
The kind of catastrophic breaches seen in New Orleans sounded a wake-up call to people living in levee-protected areas around the world.

43
00:03:19,181 --> 00:03:27,620
This was especially true for Sacramento, the capital of California, and home to 450,000.

44
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:30,900
New Orleans and Sacramento have a lot in common.

45
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:38,000
Concentrated development in the shadow of levees, prone to very large storms that produce very high water.

46
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:43,140
And when those levees fail, much of Sacramento would be at depths of 20 feet or more,

47
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:45,200
which is very similar to what you saw in New Orleans.

48
00:03:45,201 --> 00:03:50,240
Sacramento was built at the confluence of the Sacramento and American rivers,

49
00:03:50,241 --> 00:03:54,080
both of which have a long history of spilling over their banks.

50
00:03:54,081 --> 00:03:59,080
The entire city is surrounded by an earthen levee system,

51
00:03:59,081 --> 00:04:02,560
designed to keep it from flooding during winter and spring rains.

52
00:04:02,561 --> 00:04:07,320
Sacramento experienced its first flood in 1850,

53
00:04:07,321 --> 00:04:11,320
when the rivers overtopped the three-foot-tall levees that were built to contain them.

54
00:04:11,321 --> 00:04:19,540
In the ensuing years, breaches and overtopping frequently put many parts of Sacramento underwater.

55
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:29,160
In 1986, levee breaches south of the city stranded thousands of motorists on Interstate 5.

56
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:35,500
But these floods pale in comparison to the threat Sacramento currently faces.

57
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:44,920
not only are Sacramento's levies in need of upgrades and repairs but an increase in the

58
00:04:44,921 --> 00:04:50,200
frequency and intensity of storms in the past 50 years has pushed the system to its limits

59
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:57,160
a breach along the American River north of downtown during high rains

60
00:04:57,161 --> 00:05:00,160
could put the city center under 10 feet of water

61
00:05:00,161 --> 00:05:08,920
The biggest threat to Sacramento's levees comes from seepage, in which water moves through

62
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:12,540
or under the levee, weakening it to the point of collapse.

63
00:05:12,541 --> 00:05:18,880
When you have high water events, you tend to have seepage problems, and the system right

64
00:05:18,881 --> 00:05:24,960
now has been stressed for quite some time, and frankly it's something that we're quite

65
00:05:24,961 --> 00:05:27,880
concerned about.

66
00:05:27,881 --> 00:05:33,080
Nearly at risk is the pocket, a densely populated neighborhood located within a bend of the

67
00:05:33,081 --> 00:05:35,580
Sacramento River.

68
00:05:35,581 --> 00:05:40,200
The homes here are built right up against the levees, levees that are showing signs

69
00:05:40,201 --> 00:05:44,040
of erosion.

70
00:05:44,041 --> 00:05:48,880
Some pocket residents have tried to reinforce the levee with chunks of broken concrete.

71
00:05:48,881 --> 00:05:56,000
We got an attempt by some local folks to somehow halt what erosion's gone on by just throwing

72
00:05:56,001 --> 00:05:58,040
whatever debris they had available at the time.

73
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:01,040
We're standing on top of the levee,

74
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:03,360
and you can see by the high river flows

75
00:06:03,361 --> 00:06:05,820
the dynamic quality that it possesses.

76
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:08,360
In the event that the levee breached right here,

77
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:10,400
this house and the house to my right

78
00:06:10,401 --> 00:06:12,860
would probably be gone in a matter of minutes,

79
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:16,220
leaving nothing but a hundred-foot deep scour hole.

80
00:06:16,221 --> 00:06:18,540
The people would be lucky to survive.

81
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:21,980
The aftermath of a levee breach here

82
00:06:21,981 --> 00:06:25,020
would resemble this decimated neighborhood in New Orleans.

83
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,360
There is no force that can actually deal with the power of water flowing through a levee.

84
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:36,660
And so it tends to erase any structures which are right next to the levee because of the power of it.

85
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:41,020
California's levee problems aren't confined to Sacramento.

86
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:49,860
The city's levees are part of an extensive 6,000-mile system that has seen hundreds of breaches over the past 20 years.

87
00:06:49,861 --> 00:06:55,780
another area where the levees are teetering on the brink of disaster

88
00:06:55,781 --> 00:06:58,900
is the sacramento san joaquin river delta

89
00:06:58,901 --> 00:07:05,460
the delta is created by the confluence of the sacramento and san joaquin rivers

90
00:07:05,461 --> 00:07:08,300
which then drains into san francisco bay

91
00:07:08,301 --> 00:07:16,000
the delta is where the fresh water supply for 23 million people roughly two-thirds of the state

92
00:07:16,001 --> 00:07:20,640
California. It all moves through the Delta.

93
00:07:20,641 --> 00:07:27,560
The Delta is a maze of canals and man-made islands used primarily for agriculture.

94
00:07:27,561 --> 00:07:31,960
Most of the islands reside below sea level, wreathed by aging earthen levees

95
00:07:31,961 --> 00:07:35,960
first constructed over a hundred years ago.

96
00:07:35,961 --> 00:07:40,160
Some of them were put together initially with Chinese labor and wicker baskets.

97
00:07:40,161 --> 00:07:45,320
On top of that, they're placed on notoriously bad foundations in some places.

98
00:07:45,321 --> 00:07:49,760
This is why the Delta levees are so prone to failure.

99
00:07:49,761 --> 00:07:58,700
On a clear day in June 2004, a levee gave way along an island called Upper Jones Tract.

100
00:07:58,701 --> 00:08:04,680
The floodwaters overtook 650 acres of agricultural land.

101
00:08:04,681 --> 00:08:08,000
20 homes were inundated.

102
00:08:08,001 --> 00:08:13,640
Saltwater from the San Francisco Bay surged into the Delta's freshwater canals,

103
00:08:13,641 --> 00:08:17,660
forcing pumping stations to shut down and threatening the state's water supply.

104
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:23,080
But this is just a small taste of what's in store for the Delta

105
00:08:23,081 --> 00:08:26,740
if California's geologic history is taken into consideration.

106
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:32,940
The big failure, which is a scary one, is they can also completely collapse during earthquakes.

107
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:35,720
They actually literally liquefy and turn to mush.

108
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:41,340
If an earthquake can do this to a steel-reinforced cement structure,

109
00:08:41,341 --> 00:08:44,640
you can imagine what it would do to one mate of Earth.

110
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:50,380
In February 2006, after 28 straight days of rain,

111
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:53,260
California Governor Arnold Schwarzenegger

112
00:08:53,261 --> 00:08:57,040
declared the state's decaying levy system was in a state of emergency.

113
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:01,080
Our levies are extremely vulnerable, as you know,

114
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:05,380
and they're literally one big earthquake or one major storm

115
00:09:05,381 --> 00:09:08,180
away from a major Katrina-type disaster.

116
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:17,940
The state has released millions of dollars in emergency funds to reinforce 29 critical erosion sites.

117
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:23,900
However, it would take many billions of dollars to upgrade all of California's levies,

118
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:28,860
money that's subject to political wrangling and may never be allocated.

119
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:32,960
I'll tell you, the problem is probably intractable.

120
00:09:32,961 --> 00:09:36,580
I don't think you can hold the Delta together for the indefinite future.

121
00:09:36,581 --> 00:09:44,720
A solution to California's levy problems might be found across the Atlantic Ocean, in the country of the Netherlands.

122
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:49,480
The Dutch are experts at hardened flood defenses.

123
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:52,260
And it doesn't get much harder than this.

124
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:55,040
The Oosterschelt barrier.

125
00:09:57,120 --> 00:09:59,000
65 concrete piers.

126
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:01,540
62 steel floodgates.

127
00:10:02,780 --> 00:10:05,440
5.6 million tons of rock armoring.

128
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:08,700
and a four-lane bridge for good measure,

129
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:13,360
all spanning two and a half miles of the Netherlands' Oosterscheld Inlet.

130
00:10:15,180 --> 00:10:19,640
At the click of a computer mouse, this fortress-like barricade drops its gates

131
00:10:19,641 --> 00:10:24,000
to stop high water from the North Sea from flooding the southern part of the country.

132
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:29,560
The Dutch have become masters at this kind of large-scale hydraulic engineering

133
00:10:29,561 --> 00:10:32,600
because the fate of their entire nation depends on it.

134
00:10:32,601 --> 00:10:40,460
The Netherlands, Holland as it's also known, is a low-lying country smaller than the state

135
00:10:40,461 --> 00:10:46,060
of West Virginia. It fronts the North Sea to the west and holds the drainage deltas

136
00:10:46,061 --> 00:10:48,426
for three major European rivers.

137
00:10:48,427 --> 00:10:52,640
The Netherlands literally means the lowlands, with nearly

138
00:10:52,641 --> 00:10:56,260
50% of the dry land residing below sea level.

139
00:10:56,261 --> 00:11:00,040
To keep it dry, the entire country is ensconced

140
00:11:00,041 --> 00:11:02,720
behind an elaborate system of dikes and flood barriers.

141
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:07,420
Half of our country is below sea level.

142
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:10,080
With the biggest cities like Amsterdam, Waterdam,

143
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:11,560
they're all in the low-lying region.

144
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,820
Without coastal defenses, well, you can forget about the Netherlands.

145
00:11:16,100 --> 00:11:18,120
The most eminent threat to the Netherlands

146
00:11:18,121 --> 00:11:20,760
comes from the violent North Sea storm surges.

147
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:25,600
A storm surge is a rush of water driven by high winds.

148
00:11:25,601 --> 00:11:30,080
Surges are usually associated with typhoons and hurricanes

149
00:11:30,081 --> 00:11:33,260
and are most destructive during high tides.

150
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:39,180
The Oosterschelt barrier was created as a forward defense shield against surges.

151
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:45,180
All operations are managed from within its bunker-like control center,

152
00:11:45,640 --> 00:11:50,300
where 385 separate computers manage every aspect of the structure.

153
00:11:50,301 --> 00:11:59,660
Here, technicians monitor sea conditions using a system of 30 wave height sensors, positioned across the North Sea.

154
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:06,500
If the sensors predict a surge of roughly 10 feet, an operator will close the gates.

155
00:12:07,260 --> 00:12:10,580
If a human fails to flip the switch, a computer does it.

156
00:12:10,581 --> 00:12:20,620
When the decision is made to close off the inlet, massive hydraulic cylinders slowly lower the gates into position, blocking the surge.

157
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:24,000
The process takes 80 minutes to complete.

158
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:32,680
Each gate measures 137 feet wide, and they vary in height depending on the depth of the inlet.

159
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:37,260
The tallest is 40 feet high and weighs 540 tons.

160
00:12:37,261 --> 00:12:45,420
The barrier has been called into service 23 times since it opened in 1986 and is built

161
00:12:45,421 --> 00:12:50,320
to remain operative for 200 years.

162
00:12:50,321 --> 00:12:54,820
The American Society of Civil Engineers has named the Oosterschelt barrier one of the

163
00:12:54,821 --> 00:12:57,880
seven wonders of the modern world.

164
00:12:57,881 --> 00:13:03,160
Although futuristic by most standards, the Oosterschelt barrier represents 25-year-old

165
00:13:03,161 --> 00:13:04,061
technology.

166
00:13:04,062 --> 00:13:10,940
And since its completion, the Dutch have devised even more visionary ways to control

167
00:13:10,941 --> 00:13:11,641
water.

168
00:13:11,642 --> 00:13:21,360
The popular American folk song, I've been working on the railroad, was actually derived from

169
00:13:21,361 --> 00:13:26,114
an Irish folk song called I've been working on the levee.

170
00:13:26,115 --> 00:13:29,200
Levees will return on Modern Marvels.

171
00:13:33,160 --> 00:13:47,160
In the Netherlands, levees are called dikes, and there are more than 1,500 miles of them along canals, riverbanks, and the coastline.

172
00:13:49,720 --> 00:13:58,100
Add a system of storm surge barriers across inlets to the North Sea, and the entire country is encircled by a watertight defense sheet.

173
00:13:58,101 --> 00:14:05,100
But until 1997, the Netherlands still had one vulnerable opening to a storm surge.

174
00:14:05,101 --> 00:14:10,100
A shipping channel, called the New Waterway.

175
00:14:10,101 --> 00:14:16,100
The 1,100-foot wide channel leads to the port of Rotterdam, one of the busiest ports on Earth.

176
00:14:16,101 --> 00:14:23,100
The location required a barrier that would allow massive ships to pass, while still providing surge protection.

177
00:14:23,101 --> 00:14:30,884
protection. The solution was simple in conception but complex in execution.

178
00:14:30,885 --> 00:14:32,960
Two gargantuan gates

179
00:14:32,961 --> 00:14:38,200
that can swing out into the waterway when needed and stay completely out of the way when not.

180
00:14:39,460 --> 00:14:43,580
It's called the Moselent barrier and everything about it is enormous.

181
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:51,560
Each A-shaped arm is 777 feet in length, longer than two and a half football fields,

182
00:14:51,561 --> 00:14:54,620
and uses twice as much steel as the Eiffel Tower.

183
00:14:55,580 --> 00:14:58,800
The massive retaining walls soars seven stories high,

184
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:01,420
and each is 690 feet in length.

185
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:05,560
The arms and retaining walls pivot into the waterway

186
00:15:05,561 --> 00:15:09,940
on the world's largest ball joints, each 35 feet in diameter.

187
00:15:11,740 --> 00:15:14,060
We're at the back here, at the ball joint.

188
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:16,160
It's made like a human shoulder.

189
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:21,000
It works the same, because you have to make three movements at the same time.

190
00:15:21,560 --> 00:15:26,720
Vertical, horizontal, and also a turning point.

191
00:15:26,721 --> 00:15:33,300
Because there are a lot of huge weights on top of new waterway during a closing procedure.

192
00:15:35,160 --> 00:15:40,760
Protected within its own climate-controlled chamber, the ball joint is an engineering marvel unto itself.

193
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:48,580
It has to operate with smooth precision while absorbing a possible 70,000 tons of storm surge pressure.

194
00:15:48,581 --> 00:15:52,040
built in the Czech Republic.

195
00:15:52,820 --> 00:15:55,700
It's three times as large as the biggest ball joint ever created.

196
00:15:58,080 --> 00:16:01,080
Extending out from each ball joint is a web-like truss

197
00:16:01,081 --> 00:16:02,620
that supports the retaining wall.

198
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:07,980
The tubular struts were rolled out of three-inch thick steel plating.

199
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:13,680
Welders had to work in special temperature and moisture-controlled tents

200
00:16:13,681 --> 00:16:16,020
to ensure the integrity of each weld.

201
00:16:16,021 --> 00:16:20,940
To limit the amount the barrier expands under the hot sun,

202
00:16:21,720 --> 00:16:26,060
a special ingredient was embedded in the 10,000 gallons of paint that cover it.

203
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:27,560
Glass.

204
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:30,080
That's a special chosen paint.

205
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:33,520
We put small pieces of glass into the paint.

206
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:37,740
More sunlight reflection means less heating up of the steel.

207
00:16:41,740 --> 00:16:45,340
Operators man the Maslant barrier's control center around the clock.

208
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:55,220
Computers can predict 24 hours in advance if the water is expected to reach the 3 meters, or roughly 10 feet, required to close off the waterway.

209
00:16:56,320 --> 00:17:03,900
Unlike the Oosterschelt barrier, where an operator intervenes to lower the gates, human beings are taken out of the equation at the Mosslaut barrier.

210
00:17:04,840 --> 00:17:12,560
It goes all automatically, and a computer makes the decision to close the barrier, and another computer closes the barrier.

211
00:17:12,561 --> 00:17:16,560
And of course, for every computer we have a second system.

212
00:17:16,561 --> 00:17:18,560
If one fails, the other goes through.

213
00:17:18,561 --> 00:17:22,560
And if all system fails, we have always operators who can do it manual.

214
00:17:22,561 --> 00:17:25,560
But the barrier will close eventually.

215
00:17:25,561 --> 00:17:31,560
When it's time to close the gates, the dry docks holding the retaining walls are flooded.

216
00:17:31,561 --> 00:17:35,560
This causes the hollow walls to float.

217
00:17:35,561 --> 00:17:43,760
The walls are then moved into the waterway by means of four massive gears located in a housing high atop the structure.

218
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:54,140
Working like a giant bicycle chain, the high-grade chromium nickel gears engage a tooth rack, which curves along the entire length of the wall.

219
00:17:56,120 --> 00:18:00,160
This is the mechanism that moves the gates into the river.

220
00:18:00,161 --> 00:18:08,820
The movement is made by electrical hydraulic engines and they turn the tooth wheels into the tooth rack.

221
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:15,100
Once the gates reach the closed position, valves on the bottom of the walls open,

222
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:18,760
allowing 10,000 gallons of water to fill each wall.

223
00:18:20,060 --> 00:18:25,520
As the walls sink, they come to rest on a giant concrete sill across the bottom of the channel.

224
00:18:25,521 --> 00:18:34,520
The hollow walls are built just like a submarine, a big submarine.

225
00:18:34,521 --> 00:18:40,520
To get it up to the surface, you have a huge amount of water pumps inside to pump out the water.

226
00:18:40,521 --> 00:18:47,520
With the introduction of advanced super defenses like the Maasland and Oosterschelt barriers,

227
00:18:47,521 --> 00:18:54,520
the levee has come a long way from its humble beginnings as a pile of dirt on the banks of the River Nile.

228
00:18:54,521 --> 00:19:00,240
Maybe 4,000 or more years ago, we began to populate areas next to rivers.

229
00:19:00,241 --> 00:19:05,860
And we said, well, one of the ways to keep the water from coming from the river over to where we're living

230
00:19:05,861 --> 00:19:09,300
is to build this earth levee higher and higher.

231
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:12,700
And so that's where they got started, in fact, back in Egypt.

232
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:17,560
Ancient civilizations living and farming along the rivers of China,

233
00:19:18,340 --> 00:19:22,380
as well as those in the Indus River Valley in what is today India and Pakistan,

234
00:19:22,381 --> 00:19:26,840
also built levees for irrigation, transportation, and flood control.

235
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:32,020
But as long as there have been levees, there have been levee failures.

236
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:35,740
The Chinese have been battling water for years,

237
00:19:35,900 --> 00:19:40,280
and the Yellow River is particularly one of the dangerous ones.

238
00:19:41,560 --> 00:19:47,520
Since 600 B.C., the Chinese have recorded over 1,500 levee breaches along the Yellow River.

239
00:19:47,521 --> 00:19:57,180
On China's Yangtze River, a 1931 levee breach is estimated to have killed 3.7 million people.

240
00:19:58,740 --> 00:20:04,060
But it was the Dutch who truly brought levee engineering and water control into the modern age.

241
00:20:04,600 --> 00:20:06,340
They didn't just build levees.

242
00:20:07,020 --> 00:20:09,540
They used levees to build an entire nation.

243
00:20:09,541 --> 00:20:19,540
That process began when early settlers averted floodwaters by building mounds of earth, called turpen, then erected homes and villages on top of them.

244
00:20:19,541 --> 00:20:30,439
As the Dutch built more and more turpen, they connected the mounds with dykes, then pumped out the marshlands between them to create what are called polders, areas of

245
00:20:30,440 --> 00:20:32,540
land that used to be underwater.

246
00:20:32,541 --> 00:20:39,540
In the early 1600s, the Dutch developed wind power to pump water out of the polders.

247
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:47,540
Today in the Netherlands, the wind is used to make electricity.

248
00:20:47,541 --> 00:20:53,540
And the electricity powers modern pumping plants that do the same job as the original windmills.

249
00:20:53,541 --> 00:20:55,540
The Netherlands is an artificial land.

250
00:20:55,541 --> 00:20:59,540
But we are pumping out water every day, every night, constantly.

251
00:20:59,541 --> 00:21:06,540
And if you stop pumping, then in 60 hours or 80 hours, one third of the country will be wet again.

252
00:21:07,540 --> 00:21:11,540
For centuries, the Dutch lived and prospered in their artificially dry country.

253
00:21:12,540 --> 00:21:20,540
Then in 1916, a North Sea storm caused massive flooding in the lands around a saltwater inlet called the Zuiderzee.

254
00:21:20,541 --> 00:21:26,140
Zootersee. The Dutch responded by closing off the Zootersee from the North Sea with

255
00:21:26,141 --> 00:21:31,701
a 19-mile dike, the longest structure of its kind ever built.

256
00:21:31,702 --> 00:21:33,980
Completed in 1933, it has

257
00:21:33,981 --> 00:21:36,341
never been breached or overtopped.

258
00:21:36,342 --> 00:21:40,160
That success provided the Dutch with a sense of mastery

259
00:21:40,161 --> 00:21:46,220
over water, a sense that was horrifically shattered in the winter of 1953.

260
00:21:50,540 --> 00:21:53,160
In what can be considered the Katrina of the Netherlands,

261
00:21:53,580 --> 00:21:57,000
a powerful North Sea storm slammed into the southern part of the country,

262
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:00,680
smashed open dikes, and caused catastrophic flooding

263
00:22:00,681 --> 00:22:03,100
that killed more than 1,800 people.

264
00:22:05,680 --> 00:22:08,380
It's hard to imagine nowadays what happened.

265
00:22:09,040 --> 00:22:12,580
A lot of the dikes were simply too low, so they simply flooded.

266
00:22:13,060 --> 00:22:15,420
All lines of communication were cut through,

267
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:19,760
so nobody knew exactly what was happening that particular night.

268
00:22:19,761 --> 00:22:22,860
and that's the reason why a lot of Dutch people simply drowned.

269
00:22:23,560 --> 00:22:27,680
After the 53 floodings, it was quite clear that our government had to act.

270
00:22:28,240 --> 00:22:33,080
The Dutch implemented a 30-year plan to construct 13 large-scale structures

271
00:22:33,081 --> 00:22:35,700
to protect against storm surges.

272
00:22:36,320 --> 00:22:38,020
They called it the Delta Works,

273
00:22:38,200 --> 00:22:42,500
and the first completed project was this 1958 double floodgate

274
00:22:42,501 --> 00:22:44,300
along the Dutch Eisel River.

275
00:22:45,780 --> 00:22:49,660
The largest of all the Delta Works projects was the Oosterschelt Barrier,

276
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:53,680
The most recent and final, the Maaslund barrier.

277
00:22:55,360 --> 00:23:00,160
The Netherlands will never forget what happened on that cold January night in 1953.

278
00:23:01,160 --> 00:23:06,380
Since vowing never to let it happen again, not a single Hollander has been killed in a flood.

279
00:23:07,540 --> 00:23:10,320
I'm living in this area since 1965.

280
00:23:10,920 --> 00:23:16,000
And during a stormy night, you would go biking through the polars.

281
00:23:16,001 --> 00:23:20,100
you would have seen all lights switched on in the farmers' houses.

282
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:22,580
Why? They knew about the flooding in 1953.

283
00:23:22,780 --> 00:23:23,780
It might happen again.

284
00:23:24,200 --> 00:23:27,940
If you would do the same right now, all lights are switched out

285
00:23:27,941 --> 00:23:29,380
because people feel safe.

286
00:23:29,460 --> 00:23:30,780
They know we have the Delta project.

287
00:23:32,220 --> 00:23:34,060
The Dutch may have been first,

288
00:23:34,380 --> 00:23:37,940
but they're certainly not the only nation to make levees with machines

289
00:23:37,941 --> 00:23:41,480
to create a new breed of modern flood control structures.

290
00:23:41,481 --> 00:23:53,974
During the 1953 flood disaster in the Netherlands, a riverboat owner plugged a breach in a dike by navigating his ship into the opening, sparing two provinces from

291
00:23:53,975 --> 00:23:55,660
catastrophic flooding.

292
00:23:56,860 --> 00:24:01,460
Levees will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

293
00:24:01,461 --> 00:24:12,200
The same 1953 North Sea storm that marked a major turning point in flood planning for

294
00:24:12,201 --> 00:24:15,380
the Netherlands also slammed into southern England.

295
00:24:16,580 --> 00:24:22,160
A deadly surge plowed up the Thames River, reaching levees, flooding homes and farms,

296
00:24:22,600 --> 00:24:24,520
and killing more than 300 people.

297
00:24:25,820 --> 00:24:29,920
Floodwaters stopped just short of London, averting a major urban disaster.

298
00:24:29,921 --> 00:24:33,740
Fundamentally, a levee is pretty boring.

299
00:24:34,200 --> 00:24:35,160
It's a pile of dirt.

300
00:24:35,720 --> 00:24:40,280
Until you recognize what happens potentially when that pile of dirt fails.

301
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:43,700
The United Kingdom made up their mind like the Netherlands.

302
00:24:44,020 --> 00:24:46,060
And at that point, they say never again.

303
00:24:47,580 --> 00:24:51,840
The result of saying never again was the creation of this gleaming structure.

304
00:24:52,540 --> 00:24:54,320
The Thames' movable barrier.

305
00:24:54,860 --> 00:24:57,700
The world's second largest mechanical flood barrier

306
00:24:57,701 --> 00:24:59,920
after the Oosterschelt gates in the Netherlands.

307
00:25:01,480 --> 00:25:05,320
Spanning the 1,700-foot-wide river 10 miles from central London,

308
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:08,920
the barrier regulates and stops North Sea surge tides

309
00:25:08,921 --> 00:25:10,420
that can sweep up the Thames.

310
00:25:11,720 --> 00:25:15,940
It does this through the use of 10 massive semicircular steel gates

311
00:25:15,941 --> 00:25:18,360
weighing 4,000 tons each.

312
00:25:19,880 --> 00:25:23,360
When not needed, the gates lay out of the way on the riverbed.

313
00:25:23,361 --> 00:25:30,760
If a tidal surge is expected, hydraulics are used to pivot the gates upward, blocking rising waters.

314
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:39,660
The equipment used to operate each gate is housed in stainless steel shells designed to be pleasing to the eye.

315
00:25:41,180 --> 00:25:46,880
Since it went into operation in 1982, the authorities have raised the gates more than 80 times.

316
00:25:46,881 --> 00:25:51,820
But he didn't build dams or just put a big lock across it

317
00:25:51,821 --> 00:25:56,100
because he wanted to continue the water flow to and from the temps,

318
00:25:56,280 --> 00:26:00,060
as well as to allow commerce to continue.

319
00:26:00,500 --> 00:26:03,600
So by constructing the works in the way they did,

320
00:26:03,940 --> 00:26:07,140
they were able to maintain water and maintain commerce.

321
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:10,060
So a marvelous solution to a complex problem.

322
00:26:11,200 --> 00:26:16,460
An even more complex problem exists in Italy's world-famous city of canals.

323
00:26:16,880 --> 00:26:17,880
Venice.

324
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:24,880
Not only is this mecca of art, culture, and history

325
00:26:24,881 --> 00:26:27,880
slowly sinking into the Adriatic Sea,

326
00:26:27,881 --> 00:26:29,880
but sea levels are also rising.

327
00:26:31,880 --> 00:26:35,880
The city often floods 50 to 100 times a year,

328
00:26:35,881 --> 00:26:38,880
forcing Venetians to come up with creative ways to cope.

329
00:26:40,880 --> 00:26:42,880
Venice is essentially a very low-lying area

330
00:26:42,881 --> 00:26:45,880
that's situated on the Adriatic,

331
00:26:45,881 --> 00:26:55,620
connected through lagoons and waterways so that the city is essentially an island now out in almost open water.

332
00:26:57,260 --> 00:27:03,720
Venice is unique among cities in that it consists entirely of structures built atop wood pilings over water.

333
00:27:05,800 --> 00:27:12,160
Nestled within the 350 square mile Venice Lagoon, it possesses no actual land above water level.

334
00:27:12,161 --> 00:27:20,400
with no place to build protective levees or dikes it sits defenseless against the shifting tides

335
00:27:20,401 --> 00:27:28,180
the floodwaters in venice are eventually going to destroy the entire city and they do it in a

336
00:27:28,181 --> 00:27:32,900
variety of ways the first way is they begin destroying the foundations of the structures

337
00:27:32,901 --> 00:27:38,560
water also begins to percolate up through everything and eventually that will destroy

338
00:27:38,561 --> 00:27:42,480
the buildings unless somehow we're able to interrupt that process.

339
00:27:44,580 --> 00:27:51,640
In November 1966, Venetians experienced a painful wake-up call when a surge pushed water

340
00:27:51,641 --> 00:27:54,300
into the city up to a height of six feet.

341
00:27:54,301 --> 00:27:57,480
The flood caused $100 million in property damage.

342
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:04,740
When considering a flood barrier, the central issue is how not to destroy the lagoon in which

343
00:28:04,741 --> 00:28:05,851
the city resides.

344
00:28:05,852 --> 00:28:10,820
Because Venice still pours raw liquid sewage into its canals, it's important

345
00:28:10,821 --> 00:28:16,300
that the cleansing tides not be impeded for long periods.

346
00:28:16,301 --> 00:28:19,280
In 2003, the Italian government

347
00:28:19,281 --> 00:28:25,260
officially began work on an eight-year, four-billion-dollar plan to protect Venice from high tide surges.

348
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:30,800
They named it the Moses Project, after the biblical hero who parted the Red Sea.

349
00:28:30,801 --> 00:28:38,440
The city is protected from the Adriatic Sea by a series of long, narrow islands that contain three small inlets.

350
00:28:41,980 --> 00:28:48,280
The first step has already been completed, building massive breakwaters on the seaside of each inlet.

351
00:28:50,320 --> 00:28:59,420
The next phase calls for the installation of hollow, hinged flood doors filled with seawater that rest within recessed docks across the opening of each inlet.

352
00:29:00,800 --> 00:29:08,520
If a high tide surge over 40 inches is predicted, compressed air will be injected into the doors.

353
00:29:09,340 --> 00:29:14,200
This will force out the water, causing the doors to float and emerge above the surface.

354
00:29:15,740 --> 00:29:23,860
Each of the 79 doors will be 66 feet wide, 16 feet thick, and the tallest will be roughly the height of a 10-story building.

355
00:29:23,861 --> 00:29:32,020
when complete the Moses gates are expected to part the sea five to seven times a year

356
00:29:32,021 --> 00:29:38,140
by only raising them at the most critical times the ebb and flow of tides will be scarcely affected

357
00:29:38,141 --> 00:29:45,660
hardened defenses like the Moses gates in the Thames barrier are great leaps forward in storm

358
00:29:45,661 --> 00:29:53,440
surge protection but no barricade is impenetrable nature is always capable of producing storms

359
00:29:53,441 --> 00:29:58,700
that can overwhelm flood defense systems with catastrophic consequences.

360
00:30:02,300 --> 00:30:04,840
On a foggy morning in 1997,

361
00:30:04,841 --> 00:30:08,760
a dredger plowed into one of the main gates of the Thames River barrier.

362
00:30:09,720 --> 00:30:15,840
The 350-foot ship sank, but the barrier suffered no operational damage.

363
00:30:16,780 --> 00:30:18,860
Levees will return on Modern Marvels.

364
00:30:23,440 --> 00:30:30,440
In Europe, state-of-the-art levee engineering has produced a series of spectacularly successful

365
00:30:30,441 --> 00:30:34,440
flood control barriers.

366
00:30:34,441 --> 00:30:37,440
But each was born out of painful floods in the past.

367
00:30:37,441 --> 00:30:44,040
There have been some lessons learned, hard-won ones, in many parts of the world about protecting

368
00:30:44,041 --> 00:30:46,080
yourself against water.

369
00:30:46,081 --> 00:30:51,500
The Netherlands, Venice, the Thames barriers, you can look at those and see what you can

370
00:30:51,501 --> 00:30:57,460
do when you resolve that it won't happen, that you know how to do this in a good way.

371
00:30:57,461 --> 00:31:06,620
If you want to look at an area where you didn't do that, look at New Orleans.

372
00:31:06,621 --> 00:31:12,800
We knew immediately that this was probably the largest catastrophic failure of an engineered

373
00:31:12,801 --> 00:31:17,400
system in the history of the United States.

374
00:31:17,401 --> 00:31:24,200
When Hurricane Katrina swept through New Orleans, it left in its wake $20 billion in damages

375
00:31:24,201 --> 00:31:26,720
and nearly 1,300 deaths.

376
00:31:26,721 --> 00:31:32,460
Seventy-five percent of those were people over the age of 65.

377
00:31:32,461 --> 00:31:39,960
The powerful storm punched more than 50 breaches in New Orleans' 350-mile levee system.

378
00:31:39,961 --> 00:31:46,000
The flooding was exacerbated by the city's topography, which mirrors that of the Netherlands.

379
00:31:46,001 --> 00:31:49,280
Eighty percent of New Orleans lies either at or below sea level.

380
00:31:50,040 --> 00:31:51,880
And is it constant threat from water?

381
00:31:54,240 --> 00:31:58,360
There have been levees protecting New Orleans for more than 350 years.

382
00:31:59,360 --> 00:32:02,580
But the current system was designed and constructed in phases

383
00:32:02,581 --> 00:32:06,760
after Hurricane Betsy overtopped several levees in 1965,

384
00:32:07,560 --> 00:32:09,900
causing numerous deaths and extensive flooding.

385
00:32:12,500 --> 00:32:14,780
Sections of the network were still incomplete

386
00:32:14,781 --> 00:32:17,400
when Katrina hit 40 years later.

387
00:32:18,660 --> 00:32:21,160
Some of the most catastrophic breaches occurred

388
00:32:21,161 --> 00:32:24,320
when Katrina's winds drove huge volumes of water

389
00:32:24,321 --> 00:32:25,560
across Lake Pontchartrain

390
00:32:25,561 --> 00:32:29,860
and then into the canals that extend deep into the city.

391
00:32:32,100 --> 00:32:33,700
Pressure from the storm surge

392
00:32:33,701 --> 00:32:35,740
caused four levee sections to collapse,

393
00:32:36,740 --> 00:32:38,780
one along the Lower Ninth Ward,

394
00:32:39,580 --> 00:32:41,500
another along the 17th Street Canal,

395
00:32:41,501 --> 00:32:44,940
and two on the London Avenue Canal.

396
00:32:45,840 --> 00:32:48,720
Well, we're seeing an erosion feature that started up there.

397
00:32:48,820 --> 00:32:50,080
The water's coming down here.

398
00:32:50,200 --> 00:32:52,540
It drops down, and that's what ate that big hole.

399
00:32:53,400 --> 00:32:55,880
Robert B. was a member of an investigative team

400
00:32:55,881 --> 00:32:57,960
funded by the National Science Foundation.

401
00:32:59,340 --> 00:33:04,060
We started focusing on trying to understand how this failure occurred.

402
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:08,600
You could tell immediately that there had been some unusual movements

403
00:33:08,601 --> 00:33:10,760
of the soil levee itself.

404
00:33:11,500 --> 00:33:18,500
The levees that held back the canal waters were of a design known as eye walls.

405
00:33:18,501 --> 00:33:30,824
The eye walls were constructed by driving sections of steel sheet piling approximately 20 feet long into the core of the earthen levees through layers of sand, peat

406
00:33:30,825 --> 00:33:31,525
and clay.

407
00:33:31,526 --> 00:33:38,500
The pilings create a watertight retaining wall that extends three feet above the top of the levee.

408
00:33:38,501 --> 00:33:44,220
A six-foot-tall concrete flood wall was later poured over the top of the pilings.

409
00:33:47,560 --> 00:33:52,340
Discovering why the eye walls failed became a geotechnical detective story.

410
00:33:53,400 --> 00:34:01,200
And to help solve the mystery, the Army Corps of Engineers constructed a massive concrete model of the 17th Street Outflow Canal

411
00:34:01,201 --> 00:34:04,740
at their hydraulics test facility in Vicksburg, Mississippi.

412
00:34:04,741 --> 00:34:11,240
We're trying to understand why the flood walls failed in the 17th Street Canal,

413
00:34:11,460 --> 00:34:16,060
and in order to do that, we need to know what forces were happening during the hurricane.

414
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:21,540
The model uses mechanical wave generators to produce effects

415
00:34:21,541 --> 00:34:25,600
accurately mimicking Katrina's surge as it moved across Lake Pontchartrain.

416
00:34:26,880 --> 00:34:29,560
So our primary purpose is to measure wave heights,

417
00:34:29,660 --> 00:34:32,160
and we can scale these to what really happened in nature.

418
00:34:32,161 --> 00:34:37,880
Sensors placed throughout the model measure the velocity and pulse of the water

419
00:34:37,881 --> 00:34:43,260
as it surges into the canal, flows under the Hammond Highway Bridge,

420
00:34:43,640 --> 00:34:45,980
and impacts the breach site.

421
00:34:47,100 --> 00:34:50,680
The model itself has shown that the water level was up to about 9 feet,

422
00:34:51,100 --> 00:34:55,040
and then on top of that we have a wave coming in there about 1 foot high,

423
00:34:55,340 --> 00:34:58,560
so that gives us a pulsing action every 6 or 7 seconds.

424
00:34:58,561 --> 00:35:06,760
The hydraulics team collected data from their surge tests and turned them over to the Army Corps' geotechnical experts.

425
00:35:09,440 --> 00:35:16,800
Technicians constructed a 1 to 50 scale model of the 17th Street Canal levy, using actual peat soil taken from the breach site.

426
00:35:18,420 --> 00:35:22,900
The eyewall itself is represented by a quarter-inch thick plate of aluminum.

427
00:35:22,901 --> 00:35:29,900
For their test, the scientists will use one of the most powerful centrifuges on Earth.

428
00:35:29,901 --> 00:35:36,900
After the model was installed in the centrifuge, video cameras allowed technicians to watch

429
00:35:36,901 --> 00:35:40,900
and tape the results of the experiment as the machine was spinning.

430
00:35:40,901 --> 00:35:48,900
Soils depend on defining stress to get their characterization to show how they respond.

431
00:35:48,901 --> 00:35:56,680
And so by spinning this up, we are able to represent those stress levels that the soil would actually see at 17th Street.

432
00:35:57,940 --> 00:36:05,120
Because the soil test model was built 1 50th the size of the actual levee, it possessed just 1 50th the weight.

433
00:36:06,060 --> 00:36:12,920
To increase the weight to normal levels, the centrifuge generates 50 Gs, or 50 times the normal gravity.

434
00:36:12,921 --> 00:36:20,580
as the centrifuge pushed water against the eyewall model at the same relative pressures

435
00:36:20,581 --> 00:36:26,460
as Katrina's storm surge the test showed that a gap formed between the canal side of the levee

436
00:36:26,461 --> 00:36:33,480
and the eyewall once the gap opened more water pushed against the eyewall causing it to lean over

437
00:36:33,481 --> 00:36:42,120
this gap in the eyewall initiated the failure when combined with weak soils this is what caused all

438
00:36:42,121 --> 00:36:45,375
four canal walls to collapse and breach.

439
00:36:45,376 --> 00:36:49,200
The entire thing erupts and lets the water come

440
00:36:49,201 --> 00:36:51,218
crashing through to those homes.

441
00:36:51,219 --> 00:36:54,560
That's the reason we found water had just blasted out

442
00:36:54,561 --> 00:37:00,572
concrete masonry walls. A lot of water came through very quickly.

443
00:37:00,573 --> 00:37:02,700
In a report released in

444
00:37:02,701 --> 00:37:09,680
June 2006, the Army Corps of Engineers and a team of independent investigators concluded that the

445
00:37:09,681 --> 00:37:14,780
soils under many of the canal levees were not adequately tested prior to constructing the eye walls.

446
00:37:16,400 --> 00:37:20,000
Consequently, the eye walls were left with a marginal factor of safety,

447
00:37:20,600 --> 00:37:23,280
which Katrina exploited to devastating effect.

448
00:37:25,060 --> 00:37:30,080
To keep future storm surges from impacting the remaining eye walls along three of the canals,

449
00:37:30,580 --> 00:37:34,460
the Corps has erected floodgates where the canals meet Lake Pontchartrain.

450
00:37:34,461 --> 00:37:40,940
The structure that you see behind me are the frames for the giant closure gates.

451
00:37:40,941 --> 00:37:46,520
These gates will span the 17th Street Canal, and in each of these gates will be a one-foot-thick

